vel.\",\n      \"Minimal presence in the story.\",\n      \"Secondary to the main themes.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 3,\n    \"assessment\": true\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q104\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\n    \"objective\": \"Rhetorical Devices\",\n    \"stem\": \"Which device is used in 'Ask not what your country can do for you'?\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"Metaphor\",\n      \"Chiasmus\",\n      \"Hyperbole\",\n      \"Alliteration\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"No direct comparison is made.\",\n      \"Reversal of grammatical structures for rhetorical effect.\",\n      \"No exaggeration present.\",\n      \"Repetition of consonant sounds is minimal.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 4,\n    \"assessment\": true\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q105\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\n    \"objective\": \"Point of View\",\n    \"stem\": \"Which point of view allows the narrator to know all characters' thoughts?\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"First person\",\n      \"Second person\",\n      \"Third person limited\",\n      \"Third person omniscient\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 3,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Limited to narrator's perspective.\",\n      \"Addresses the reader directly.\",\n      \"Restricted to one character's thoughts.\",\n      \"All-knowing narrator can access any character's mind.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\n    \"assessment\": true\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q106\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\n    \"objective\": \"Citation Formats\",\n    \"stem\": \"In MLA format, where does the period go in relation to the citation?\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"Before the parenthetical citation\",\n      \"Inside the parenthetical citation\",\n      \"After the parenthetical citation\",\n      \"It doesn't matter\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Incorrect MLA format.\",\n      \"Incorrect MLA format.\",\n      \"The period always comes after the citation in MLA.\",\n      \"MLA has specific rules for punctuation.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\n    \"assessment\": true\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q107\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\n    \"objective\": \"Poetry Analysis\",\n    \"stem\": \"What is the rhyme scheme of a Shakespearean sonnet?\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"ABAB CDCD EFEF GG\",\n      \"ABBA ABBA CDC DCD\",\n      \"AABB CCDD EEFF GG\",\n      \"ABCD ABCD EFGH IJ\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Correct pattern for Shakespearean/English sonnets.\",\n      \"This is a Petrarchan sonnet pattern.\",\n      \"Not a traditional sonnet form.\",\n      \"Not a recognized sonnet pattern.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 3,\n    \"assessment\": true\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q108\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\n    \"objective\": \"Grammar Usage\",\n    \"stem\": \"Which sentence correctly uses a semicolon?\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"I love reading; especially novels.\",\n      \"The test is tomorrow; I need to study.\",\n      \"She is smart; because she reads a lot.\",\n      \"He ran quickly; to catch the bus.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Semicolon shouldn't separate a fragment.\",\n      \"Correctly joins two independent clauses.\",\n      \"'Because' creates a dependent clause.\",\n      \"'To catch' creates an infinitive phrase, not an independent clause.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 3,\n    \"assessment\": true\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q109\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\n    \"objective\": \"Tone and Mood\",\n    \"stem\": \"What is the difference between tone and mood?\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"Tone is the reader's feeling; mood is the author's attitude\",\n      \"They are the same thing\",\n      \"Tone is the author's attitude; mood is the reader's feeling\",\n      \"Tone is only in poetry; mood is only in prose\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Reversed definitions.\",\n      \"They are distinct literary concepts.\",\n      \"Tone reflects the author's perspective; mood is the emotional atmosphere created for the reader.\",\n      \"Both apply to all literary forms.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\n    \"assessment\": true\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q110\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\n    \"objective\": \"Writing Process\",\n    \"stem\": \"Which revision strategy is most effective for improving clarity?\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"Reading your work aloud\",\n      \"Checking spelling only\",\n      \"Counting words\",\n      \"Changing font size\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Hearing the text helps identify awkward phrasing and unclear passages.\",\n      \"Spelling is important but doesn't address clarity.\",\n      \"Word count doesn't improve clarity.\",\n      \"Formatting doesn't affect content clarity.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\n    \"assessment\": true\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q111\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\n    \"objective\": \"Poetry Analysis\",\n    \"stem\": \"How many lines are in a Shakespearean sonnet?\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"12 lines\",\n      \"14 lines\",\n      \"16 lines\",\n      \"18 lines\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Incorrect. 12 lines would be too short for the traditional sonnet structure.\",\n      \"Correct! Shakespearean sonnets always have 14 lines written in iambic pentameter.\",\n      \"Incorrect. 16 lines exceeds the traditional sonnet length.\",\n      \"Incorrect. 18 lines is too long for a sonnet.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\n    \"assessment\": true\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q112\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\n    \"objective\": \"Poetry Analysis\",\n    \"stem\": \"What's the rhyme scheme of a Shakespearean sonnet?\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"ABBA ABBA CDC DCD\",\n      \"ABAB CDCD EFEF GG\",\n      \"AABB CCDD EEFF GG\",\n      \"ABCD ABCD EFGH IJ\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"This is the rhyme scheme of a Petrarchan (Italian) sonnet, not Shakespearean.\",\n      \"Correct! The three quatrains follow alternating rhyme (ABAB, CDCD, EFEF), ending with a rhyming couplet (GG).\",\n      \"This pattern uses only couplets, which doesn't match the Shakespearean structure.\",\n      \"This pattern has no rhyme scheme at all and isn't a traditional sonnet form.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 3,\n    \"assessment\": true\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q113\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\n    \"objective\": \"Poetry Analysis\",\n    \"stem\": \"Where does the volta (turn in thought) typically occur in a Shakespearean sonnet?\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"At line 5\",\n      \"At line 9 or in the final couplet (line 13)\",\n      \"At the very beginning (line 1)\",\n      \"At line 8\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Too early. Line 5 is in the middle of the second quatrain, not a typical turning point.\",\n      \"Correct! The volta usually appears at the start of the third quatrain (line 9) or in the final couplet (line 13), shifting the poem's direction or offering resolution.\",\n      \"The poem typically establishes its theme first before turning, so line 1 is too early.\",\n      \"Line 8 is more common in Petrarchan sonnets, not Shakespearean structure.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 3,\n    \"assessment\": true\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q114\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\n    \"objective\": \"Poetry Analysis\",\n    \"stem\": \"Which of the following is a recurring theme in Shakespeare's sonnets?\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"Space exploration and technology\",\n      \"Love, time, beauty, and mortality\",\n      \"Political revolution\",\n      \"Agricultural practices\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Anachronistic. Shakespeare lived in the late 16th/early 17th century, long before space exploration.\",\n      \"Correct! Shakespeare's sonnets deeply explore universal themes of love, the passage of time, the nature of beauty, death, and existential questions.\",\n      \"While Shakespeare wrote political plays, his sonnets focus more on personal and philosophical themes.\",\n      \"Not a central theme in Shakespeare's sonnets, which focus on human emotion and experience.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\n    \"assessment\": true\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q115\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\n    \"objective\": \"Developmental Literacy\",\n    \"stem\": \"What's the primary focus of phonemic awareness?\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"Recognizing sentence rhythm\",\n      \"Recognizing individual sounds in words\",\n      \"Identifying prefixes and suffixes\",\n      \"Memorizing sight words\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Sentence rhythm relates to prosody, not phonemic awareness.\",\n      \"Correct! Phonemic awareness focuses on hearing, identifying, and manipulating individual sounds (phonemes) in spoken words.\",\n      \"Prefixes and suffixes are part of morphology, not phonemic awareness.\",\n      \"Sight word memorization is a separate literacy skill.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\n    \"assessment\": true\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q116\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\n    \"objective\": \"Developmental Literacy\",\n    \"stem\": \"Which skill bridges decoding and comprehension?\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"Vocabulary development\",\n      \"Phoneme blending\",\n      \"Sight word recognition\",\n      \"Oral fluency\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 3,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"While important, vocabulary is more directly related to comprehension than bridging the two.\",\n      \"Phoneme blending is a decoding skill, not a bridge between decoding and comprehension.\",\n      \"Sight words support decoding but don't bridge to comprehension.\",\n      \"Correct! Oral fluency combines accurate decoding with expression and understanding, linking both skills.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 3,\n    \"assessment\": true\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q117\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\n    \"objective\": \"Developmental Literacy\",\n    \"stem\": \"What stage comes before transitional literacy?\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"Emergent\",\n      \"Fluent\",\n      \"Early\",\n      \"Proficient\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Emergent is the first stage, coming before early, not transitional.\",\n      \"Fluent comes after transitional.\",\n      \"Correct! The progression is: Emergent → Early → Transitional → Fluent → Proficient.\",\n      \"Proficient is the final stage, after fluent.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\n    \"assessment\": true\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q118\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\n    \"objective\": \"Developmental Literacy\",\n    \"stem\": \"Why is morphology instruction beneficial?\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"It improves handwriting\",\n      \"It helps infer meaning from unfamiliar words\",\n      \"It improves sentence rhythm\",\n      \"It builds phonemic awareness\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Morphology doesn't relate to handwriting (a motor skill).\",\n      \"Correct! Understanding roots, prefixes, and suffixes allows students to decode and infer meaning from new words.\",\n      \"Sentence rhythm relates to prosody, not morphology.\",\n      \"Phonemic awareness deals with sounds, not word parts.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\n    \"assessment\": true\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q119\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\n    \"objective\": \"Developmental Literacy\",\n    \"stem\": \"What's a key strategy for struggling readers?\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"Reducing text exposure\",\n      \"Assigning silent reading only\",\n      \"Modeling fluent reading\",\n      \"Avoiding feedback\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Reducing exposure limits practice opportunities and slows progress.\",\n      \"Silent reading alone doesn't provide the modeling and support struggling readers need.\",\n      \"Correct! Modeling fluent reading demonstrates proper pacing, expression, and decoding strategies.\",\n      \"Feedback is essential for growth and correction of errors.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\n    \"assessment\": true\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q120\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\n    \"objective\": \"Developmental Literacy\",\n    \"stem\": \"What's metacognition in literacy?\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"Memorizing definitions\",\n      \"Monitoring and adjusting one's understanding\",\n      \"Identifying literary genres\",\n      \"Copying model sentences\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Memorization is a surface-level skill, not metacognition.\",\n      \"Correct! Metacognition is thinking about one's own thinking—actively monitoring comprehension and adjusting strategies.\",\n      \"Genre identification is a separate literacy skill.\",\n      \"Copying is a mechanical task, not reflective thinking.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 3,\n    \"assessment\": true\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q121\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\n    \"objective\": \"Developmental Literacy\",\n    \"stem\": \"Why is background knowledge critical for comprehension?\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"It reduces decoding time\",\n      \"It links new information to existing schema\",\n      \"It improves phonics speed\",\n      \"It supports handwriting fluency\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Background knowledge relates to meaning-making, not decoding speed.\",\n      \"Correct! Prior knowledge connects new information to existing understanding (schema), providing context for comprehension.\",\n      \"Phonics speed is unrelated to background knowledge.\",\n      \"Handwriting is a separate motor skill.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\n    \"assessment\": true\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q122\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\n    \"objective\": \"Developmental Literacy\",\n    \"stem\": \"How does syntax shape meaning?\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"Determines pronunciation\",\n      \"Influences rhythm\",\n      \"Organizes ideas and emphasis\",\n      \"Adds imagery\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Pronunciation is determined by phonology, not syntax.\",\n      \"While syntax can affect rhythm, its primary function is meaning organization.\",\n      \"Correct! Sentence structure organizes ideas, determines emphasis, and affects how information is interpreted.\",\n      \"Imagery comes from word choice (diction), not sentence structure.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 3,\n    \"assessment\": true\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q123\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\n    \"objective\": \"Developmental Literacy\",\n    \"stem\": \"What's the main purpose of literacy assessment?\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"Ranking students\",\n      \"Identifying learning needs and progress\",\n      \"Recording attendance\",\n      \"Rewarding effort\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Ranking can demotivate and doesn't guide instruction effectively.\",\n      \"Correct! Assessment diagnoses strengths and needs, guiding targeted instruction and measuring growth.\",\n      \"Attendance tracking is administrative, not instructional assessment.\",\n      \"While recognition matters, assessment's primary purpose is diagnostic and instructional.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\n    \"assessment\": true\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q124\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\n    \"objective\": \"Developmental Literacy\",\n    \"stem\": \"Which element defines fluent reading?\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"Slow, careful decoding\",\n      \"Smooth, accurate, expressive reading\",\n      \"Memorization of every word\",\n      \"Reading silently only\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Slow, careful reading indicates developing fluency, not fluent reading.\",\n      \"Correct! Fluent reading is smooth, accurate, and expressive with appropriate pacing and prosody.\",\n      \"Fluency involves automatic decoding, not memorization of all words.\",\n      \"Fluency applies to both oral and silent reading.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\n    \"assessment\": true\n  }\n];\n});","lineCount":508,"map":[[508,3]],"functionMap":null},"type":"js/module"}]}